---
/**
 * subscribed.astro — Newsletter subscription confirmation landing page.
 *
 * WHAT THIS FILE IS
 * A tiny, fully-static Astro page rendered at the route `/subscribed`. It is the
 * human-facing "thank you / you're in" screen a visitor lands on AFTER they have
 * confirmed (double opt-in) their newsletter subscription. It contains no logic —
 * it is pure presentational markup wrapped in the shared site layout.
 *
 * SINGLE RESPONSIBILITY
 * Show a friendly confirmation message and point the reader back into the blog.
 * That is the whole job. It intentionally does NOT verify, mutate, or read any
 * subscription state.
 *
 * HOW IT FITS THE ARCHITECTURE
 * The subscription flow lives elsewhere (a Pages Function / Supabase +
 * Resend-driven double opt-in). That flow is responsible for:
 *   1. Capturing the email (with a Turnstile bot check + honeypot on the form),
 *   2. Persisting a pending subscriber row in Supabase (guarded by RLS),
 *   3. Emailing a signed confirmation link via Resend,
 *   4. Verifying that link's token server-side and marking the row confirmed.
 * ONLY once step 4 succeeds should the user be redirected here. This page is the
 * terminal, side-effect-free destination of that redirect — the "receipt", not
 * the "transaction".
 *
 * SECURITY / TRUST ASSUMPTIONS (important — do not weaken)
 *   - This page performs NO authentication, NO token verification, and NO
 *     entitlement check. It must therefore never display anything sensitive
 *     (no email address, no subscriber data, no admin controls). Anyone who
 *     types the URL directly will see it — that is fine BECAUSE it reveals
 *     nothing and changes nothing.
 *   - Because it is statically pre-rendered at build time (no server runtime,
 *     no `Astro.request` usage, no cookies read), there is no injection surface
 *     and no per-request state to leak.
 *   - Corollary / gotcha: reaching this page is NOT proof that a subscription
 *     was actually confirmed. Never treat a visit here as an entitlement. The
 *     real confirmation is the Supabase row flipped by the server-side token
 *     check; this page is cosmetic.
 *
 * DEPENDENCIES (imports)
 *   - BaseLayout: the shared site chrome (html/head/meta/OG, header, footer,
 *     global styles). All page content is projected into its default <slot/>.
 *     `title` feeds <title>/OG; `description` feeds the meta description.
 *
 * WHAT DEPENDS ON THIS FILE
 *   - The server-side confirmation handler (functions/api/confirm.js) 302-
 *     redirects the browser to `/subscribed` on a successful token match. If
 *     this route is renamed or removed, update that redirect target or confirmed
 *     users will hit a 404. Its sibling failure destination is the sister page
 *     `/subscribe-error` (every failure path in confirm.js lands there instead).
 *   - Possibly the Resend confirmation email copy ("you'll be taken to…"),
 *     sent by sendConfirmationEmail() in lib/email.js.
 *
 * MAINTENANCE NOTE
 * Keep this page dumb and static. Any temptation to "read the token here and
 * show the user's email" belongs in the server function, not in this
 * publicly-reachable static page.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import site from '../data/site.json';
const c = (site as any).subscribed || {};
---

{/*
  Static confirmation content, slotted into BaseLayout.
  - title/description are passed to <head> by BaseLayout (drives <title> + OG card).
  - &#127881; is the HTML numeric entity for the 🎉 party-popper emoji; used as an
    entity (rather than a raw glyph) to stay encoding-safe in the source file.
  - &rarr; renders "→"; also an entity for the same encoding-safety reason.
  - The only interactive element is a plain internal link back to /blog — no forms,
    no scripts, no client JS ships from this page.
*/}
<BaseLayout title={c.seo?.title || 'Subscribed — areyoustillreading'} description={c.seo?.description || "You're subscribed."}>
  <h1>{c.heading || 'You’re subscribed 🎉'}</h1>
  <p>{c.body || "Thanks for confirming. You'll get new posts by email."}</p>
  <p><a href="/blog">{c.cta || 'Read the blog →'}</a></p>
</BaseLayout>
